<h2>How to compare BMW diagnostic entitlements without misleading totals</h2><h3>The unit of comparison must be the task actually unlocked</h3><p>A BMW owner may need feature coding, fault memory access, live data, battery registration or an official workshop test plan. Buying the lowest-priced product name does not solve the task unless the exact license, vehicle model and interface support it. BimmerCode's iOS Full Version is a different category from BimmerLink's diagnostic unlock, and the availability of a supported adapter for one app does not guarantee an equivalent function in the other. ProTool explicitly separates Diagnostic, Coding and Master entitlements. Carly uses subscriptions and packages whose features depend on brand and vehicle. BMW TIS/AOS is professional technical-information access, not a personal scanner application. Start with a named desired operation and verify its eligibility before assigning any numeric cost.</p>
<h3>Why the US App Store amount is not a worldwide price</h3><p>Apple's US App Store currently lists BimmerCode Full Version and BimmerLink Full Version as separate in-app purchases. Those figures establish a dated storefront claim, not worldwide or cross-platform pricing. Taxes, currency, local store tiers, account region and special purchase categories can differ. A user holding an Android phone should confirm the Google Play app and its local payment mechanism rather than extrapolate an iOS charge. Each app requires a compatible interface for practical use; even a previously purchased Bluetooth dongle is only reusable when it appears on the current supported list for the exact vehicle and operating system. This ledger preserves the US source links and labels its limits.</p>
<h3>BimmerCode and BimmerLink are not a bundled entitlement</h3><p>The two applications have complementary, not identical, primary purposes. BimmerCode focuses on documented coding options and vehicle configurations, while BimmerLink focuses on reading faults, live sensor data, logging and certain service functions. The developer's App Store descriptions explicitly distinguish what they do and qualify many BimmerLink services by factory equipment or vehicle generation. Buying one full version does not include the other; the optional BimmerLink CarPlay add-on is a third separate purchase. If the user's only goal is battery registration or DPF data, evaluate BimmerLink's supported function rather than budgeting for an unrelated comfort-coding license. If both categories are necessary, price both and verify their adapter compatibility independently.</p>
<h3>ProTool's Diagnostics, Coding and Master logic</h3><p>BimmerGeeks lists Diagnostic, Coding and Master packages with different declared scopes. The company's Diagnostic license description explicitly identifies exceptions for battery and injector coding, so a buyer should not simplify the term 'Diagnostics' into absolutely no write-capable features. Conversely, the Coding license does not necessarily include all the scanning and guided diagnostics a workshop owner expects. Master bundles both categories under the publisher's current pricing, but adapters are not included and the app requires a supported Android host. BimmerGeeks also documents older chassis excluded from coding even where diagnostic communication may be available. The correct total is license plus suitable interface and Android host, subject to vehicle eligibility, not merely the lowest visible Master price.</p>
<h3>Carly pricing requires renewal and a brand-specific quote</h3><p>Carly's own publications and help center discuss a Universal Scanner, Premium brand licenses and options for multiple vehicle brands. Public promotional blog figures can be useful background, but exact checkout prices and package structures differ by region, channel, vehicle and offer. The company has also published slightly different simplified descriptions of Basic and Premium feature scope; that is an additional reason to verify the current local checkout and feature matrix rather than force every article into a timeless package table. This ledger deliberately marks the Carly annual premium price as 'quote required' and asks the user to provide their actual quoted amount. Under an annual plan, multi-year cost is sensitive to renewals and any planned changes in ownership; a short marketing teaser is not a guaranteed three-year renewal rate.</p>
<h3>BMW AOS is professional access rather than a consumer license</h3><p>BMW North America's technical portal lists time-limited subscriptions for authorized service information and software access. Its public site specifies that North American users should use this portal, while users elsewhere should follow their regional AOS route. ISTA's fault diagnosis, guided plans, repair instructions and programming environment have separate hardware, operating system and technician prerequisites. A published one-day access price does not include an approved ICOM/J2534 vehicle interface, PC and all workshop preparation. In a cost comparison, BMW AOS belongs in an authorized workshop category, not in a consumer app cost ranking. The calculator counts a chosen access pass once rather than pretending the same person renews a day pass continuously for three years.</p>
<h3>Adapter reuse is an assumption to verify, not a universal discount</h3><p>If an owner has an OBDLink or other adapter from a previous project, the temptation is to set the hardware cost to zero for all new apps. That is legitimate only if the exact model, firmware, operating system and target vehicle remain supported by the new app. An adapter that reads standard emissions codes may still be rejected by a BMW coding app, while an ENET or ICOM path has an entirely different interface architecture. The calculator's zero-price field is a user assumption, not an independent endorsement of reusing any existing dongle. Readers should link the accepted adapter SKU and current developer list in their evidence record before treating reuse as an economic saving.</p>
<h3>Three-year math must reflect charge type, not annualize everything</h3><p>One-time in-app unlocks, annual renewable subscriptions and time-limited technical passes behave differently. For a documented one-time purchase, a longer horizon does not automatically multiply the license line; for an annual premium product, repeating the current quoted amount for two or three years is an explicit forecast assumption; and for a BMW AOS month/day pass, the actual number of passes depends on expected workshop usage. The ownership worksheet makes these charge types visible instead of multiplying every price by the selected horizon. Readers should estimate future fees, exchange rates, taxes and price changes separately from the published snapshot. No guarantee of perpetual feature compatibility follows from a one-time purchase.</p>
<h3>Optional extras can distort comparisons</h3><p>A CarPlay display add-on, an adapter included with a bundle, extra vehicle brands, AI repair assistance or additional licenses may all change the apparent cost of a software solution. But those options do not necessarily unlock the reader's required diagnostic module. For example, BimmerLink CarPlay support is an optional add-on and should only be budgeted if the reader requires those documented in-car display functions. Carly's included scanner relationship depends on the chosen package, and ProTool's Master license does not include a physical interface. The correct comparison lists hardware, host, license and options as separate lines, and marks each item required, optional or already owned.</p>
<h3>The platform and vehicle may make a cheap license unusable</h3><p>Price alone cannot establish whether an application runs on the owner's phone or supports the exact BMW. The BimmerGeeks Master license disclaimer limits ProTool to compatible Android devices and warns users to check vehicle support before buying. BimmerCode and BimmerLink publish separate lists of compatible vehicle families and OBD interfaces. Even an official app listing with broad BMW coverage may qualify special control units, manufacturer-installed equipment or specific functions. If the needed function is absent or the host does not meet requirements, the advertised price is not a relevant comparison. Verify compatibility before creating the cost worksheet; otherwise one unsupported cheap license can make the total misleading.</p>
<h3>Keep source revisions and old numbers distinguishable</h3><p>A useful pricing ledger should record product name, exact entitlement, source URL, currency, country or app storefront, reviewed date, hardware inclusion, renewal rule and functional caveats. If a provider revises a price, the ledger can add a new dated observation without pretending that the older article always used that number. The original Chassis Signal pricing guides contain research published on different August and September dates; those original words and visuals should be preserved. We add contextual links to this newer shared ledger and update substantive details only when there is first-party evidence. A historical screenshot or uncited aggregator figure should never overrule a documented current checkout.</p>
<h3>No SKU or price is a universal safety recommendation</h3><p>This resource compares entitlements, not the safety of programming or service interventions. Paying for a software tier with active tests or coding does not authorize the operator to activate safety systems, disable legal functions, rewrite ECU firmware or ignore manufacturer technical guidance. BMW's AOS user guide describes qualified workshop and diagnostic resources; ProTool and app developers also publish support exclusions and warnings. When an operation requires high-voltage isolation, stable programming supply, controlled brake procedure or professional training, the price of the license is a secondary issue. Stop until the exact vehicle procedure and responsible operator are established.</p>
<h3>A practical purchasing checklist and final decision rule</h3><p>First write the exact task and BMW chassis. Next confirm the software vendor supports that task and vehicle, and that the chosen platform and interface are approved. Then select the smallest appropriate license tier, record its dated official source and regional checkout cost, and list any required hardware or optional add-ons. Finally model renewal for the user's expected ownership horizon and keep uncertain future charges explicit. If any essential compatibility claim is missing, the correct output of the worksheet is 'not verified' regardless of an attractive advertised license price. The related original guides remain useful because they address narrower purchase decisions that the shared ledger does not replace.</p>
